"""The meetings' model writers as agents: the notes of a meeting, how to
work with each person, what a series of meetings keeps coming back to and
which of a call's questions were worth answering each run as an ``agent``
row, seeded with the prompts below, so their prompt, model and sampling
are edited in Agents like any other agent's (each change kept in its
prompt history). While a row is missing or off, its built-in prompt runs
on the model in force. A question asked in a call is answered by Illiana
herself (``actions.ask_ai``), not by one of these."""

from typing import Any

from sqlmodel import Session

NOTES_AGENT = "meeting-notes"
GUIDE_AGENT = "meeting-guide"
THEMES_AGENT = "meeting-themes"
QUESTIONS_AGENT = "meeting-questions"
# Generous: a long meeting's notes run past the chat's 1000 tokens.
MAX_TOKENS = 8000

NOTES_PROMPT = """You write the notes of a meeting from its transcript.
Each transcript line reads "[id] Speaker: what they said".

Write a short summary, the decisions made, the action items (who has to do
what, and by when if it was said), and the questions left open. Leave a
list empty rather than invent an item. Give each item the ids of the lines
it came from. An action item's owner is the speaker who has to do it,
named exactly as in the transcript, or none when nobody took it.

Then the threads, each citing the lines it came from, speakers named
exactly as in the transcript:
- Commitments: who said they would do what, for whom and by when, in
  their own words ("I'll send it to you").
- Follow-ups: when what was said before this meeting lists commitments
  still open, each one raised again here, by its [C...] number: done,
  dropped, or only mentioned and still open.
- Contradictions: something said one way and the opposite way, by one
  person or two, in this meeting or against an earlier line; the lines of
  one side, and of the other as against.
- Positions: where each person stands on the topics they spoke to, and
  where they stood before when it is a change."""

GUIDE_PROMPT = """You write how to work with one person, from what was said
in meetings they were in. Each meeting starts with its heading; each line
reads "[id] Speaker: what they said".

Write only how they work: what they care about, how they like their
information (big picture or detail, a demo or numbers, short or thorough),
how they decide and commit, and what lands with them and what gets
pushback. Read it from what they said and how they answered others.

Nothing about their life outside work: leave out health, family,
relationships, politics and religion, even when it was said. Leave a part
empty rather than guess. Give each point the ids of the lines it rests on.
The owner's own note on them, when there is one, is what the owner knows:
weigh it, but cite only lines."""


THEMES_PROMPT = """You read a series of meetings: the same meeting, held
again and again. Each meeting starts with its heading, then its summary
and the items of its notes; each line under an item reads
"[id] Speaker: what they said".

Write what the series said over time. Be brief: every point a few words
(at most twelve), like a heading, not a sentence; at most five points in
each list, the ones that matter most first; each citing the lines it
rests on.
- A summary: one sentence (at most twenty-five words) on where the series
  stands now.
- Themes: each thing raised in more than one meeting: an ask made again,
  a blocker that keeps coming back, a worry nobody resolves. Give each
  every line it was said in, across the meetings, and whether by the
  latest meeting it was answered, done, or is still open.
- Settled: each thing decided or done.
- Shifted: each position or priority that changed, as "from → to".
- Still open: each thing not yet resolved.
- Mood: how the room feels about the work.

Something said in one meeting only is not a theme. Leave a list empty
rather than invent a point. Name people exactly as the lines do."""

QUESTIONS_PROMPT = """You sort the questions heard in a meeting once it is
over. You get them as cards: each starts with its id in brackets, then the
lines said just before it and the question itself (or a few asked
together), one line each as "Speaker: what they said".

For each card give its id, whether to keep it, and its topic.

Keep a card when it asks something the system being discussed, its code,
its data or its records could answer: how something works, what it does,
a number, a date, a status. Do not keep small talk, a question put to
someone in the meeting (asking them to do something, for their opinion,
or whether they can hear), a fragment that cannot be understood on its
own, or one answered in the lines given.

The topic is what the card is about, in two to five words. Give cards
about the same thing the same topic, word for word: they are read
together."""


def _definition(slug: str, name: str, description: str, prompt: str) -> dict[str, Any]:
    return {
        "slug": slug,
        "name": name,
        "description": description,
        "category": "meetings",
        "model_id": None,
        "system_prompt": prompt,
        "temperature": 0.3,
        "max_tokens": MAX_TOKENS,
    }


def meetings_agent_definitions() -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
    return [
        _definition(
            NOTES_AGENT,
            "Meeting notes",
            "Writes a meeting's notes from its transcript",
            NOTES_PROMPT,
        ),
        _definition(
            GUIDE_AGENT,
            "How to work with them",
            "Writes how to work with each person from what they said",
            GUIDE_PROMPT,
        ),
        _definition(
            THEMES_AGENT,
            "What keeps coming up",
            "Writes what a series of meetings keeps coming back to, over time",
            THEMES_PROMPT,
        ),
        _definition(
            QUESTIONS_AGENT,
            "Questions in a call",
            "Keeps the questions worth answering from a meeting, by topic",
            QUESTIONS_PROMPT,
        ),
    ]


def load_meetings_agent_fixtures(session: Session) -> dict[str, int]:
    """Seed the meetings' agents that are missing; a row edited in Agents
    is never touched. The default agent, Overseer's chat, is granted the
    meetings search (``tools.meetings_search``)."""
    # Here, not at the top: the AI fixtures package loads these seeds.
    from app.services.ai.service import DEFAULT_AGENT_SLUG
    from app.services.ai.fixtures.agent_fixtures import (
        attach_tools,
        seed_missing_agents,
    )

    seeded = seed_missing_agents(session, meetings_agent_definitions())
    granted = attach_tools(session, DEFAULT_AGENT_SLUG, ["meetings_search"])
    if granted:
        session.commit()
    return {"meetings_agents": seeded, "meetings_tool_links": granted}
{% endif %}
